>> It gives the state of every topic? How does that work? Also it seems to
>> be branch-based, rather than patch-based.
>
> I overstated it. The report covers only topics in flight, and you are
> right that it is branch-based. When Junio picks up a series he applies
> it to a topic branch named for the author, e.g. rs/archive-zip-..., and
> merges that into seen. A reroll replaces the branch until the topic
> reaches next; after that it changes only by incremental patches.
>
> The report is generated by the Meta/cook script on his todo branch. It
> walks the topic merges in master..seen, so a topic appears once it is
> merged to seen and leaves when it graduates to master or is discarded.
> maintain-git.adoc says a topic inactive for three weeks may be dropped
> from seen, and SubmittingPatches says one with unaddressed review may
> go after four weeks. Each entry carries a "source: <message-id>" line,
> which the script recovers from notes that git am leaves on each commit.
> Here is an entry from the current issue:
>
>     * rs/archive-zip-omit-out-of-range-mtime (2026-10-12) 2 commits
>      - SQUASH??? if time_t is 64 bit ...
>      - archive-zip: omit UT extra field if mtime is out of range
>      ...
>      Expecting a reroll.
>      cf. <3a7868a2-a65d-4540-8ab1-70cfbae9754f@web.de>
>      source: <595dafac-33c7-4c45-9cac-c868cf1f8bb0@web.de>
>
> So the link runs from commit to mail, never from mail to state.
>
>> I can't imagine that git reports on every single email thread that has
>> ever existed, that would be insane.
