>>> sourcehut (lists.sr.ht) is the closest match to what you propose. A
>>> reply in a patchset thread can carry the header
>>> "X-Sourcehut-Patchset-Update:" with one of PROPOSED, NEEDS_REVISION,
>>> SUPERSEDED, APPROVED, REJECTED or APPLIED. The list sets the
>>> patchset's state from it.
>>
>> Interesting can you provide docs and examples for this? Also how does it
>> compare to other methods?
>
> The docs are one paragraph under "Email controls":
>
>     https://man.sr.ht/lists.sr.ht/#email-controls
>
>     Email replies to patchset threads can include a custom header,
>     X-Sourcehut-Patchset-Update to update the status of the patchset
>     to PROPOSED, NEEDS_REVISION, SUPERSEDED, APPROVED, REJECTED, or
>     APPLIED.
>
> They give no example. In use it is one added header on an ordinary
> reply:
>
>     In-Reply-To: <cover.123.git.someone@example.org>
>     X-Sourcehut-Patchset-Update: APPROVED
>
>     Looks good, thanks.
>
> The docs do not say who may set it, so I read the source
> (git.sr.ht/~sircmpwn/lists.sr.ht, api/lists/patchset.go and
> archive.go). A list moderator may set any state. The submitter, matched
> by From, may set only REJECTED, SUPERSEDED or NEEDS_REVISION, and only
> while the patchset is not yet applied, approved or rejected. A header
> that fails these checks is logged and ignored, and no bounce goes out.
> The web UI and "hut lists patchset update" set the same state.
>
> lists.sr.ht also supersedes on its own. When a new version arrives with
> the same subject and submitter and a higher version number, the
> previous one is marked SUPERSEDED if it was still PROPOSED or
> NEEDS_REVISION.
>
> Compared with the others:
>
> - lists.sr.ht: the state lives in its database. Mail is one way to
>   change it, and the headers stay in the archive, but the permission
>   checks and ordering live in the server.
> - Patchwork: the state lives in its own database, set in the web UI,
>   through the API or pwclient, or by a git hook that matches pushed
>   commits to patches by diff hash. Core Patchwork has a Superseded
>   state but does not set it by itself; kernel.org runs separate bots
>   for that.
> - b4 and lore: everything is mail. b4 writes the "Changes in vN" and
>   "Link to vN" lines and a change-id, and "b4 ty" sends "Applied,
>   thanks!". There is no tracker; b4 keeps its thank-you bookkeeping on
>   the maintainer's machine.
> - git.git: the state lives in the maintainer's branches, and the
>   report is generated from them and mailed out.
>
> We would be closest to b4 and lore, with a sourcehut-style control
> written as a body trailer rather than a header.

Is there any reason to bring b4 into the mix for my workflow?
